  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Necklace Tricholoma (Tricholoma focale) is a small to medium-sized mushroom characterized by its distinctive dark caps and gill arrangement that resembles a necklace. This fungus belongs to the Tricholomataceae family and is found in forested regions of New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap appearance",
      "description": "Dark brown to grayish-brown cap with a distinctive radial fibrous pattern and pale margin",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Gill arrangement",
      "description": "Whitish to pale yellow gills with darker edges that create a necklace-like pattern when viewed from below",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem characteristics",
      "description": "White to pale stem with fine fibrils and a subtle ring or ring zone near the apex",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Necklace Tricholoma typically grows in mixed deciduous and coniferous forests of New England, often fruiting in autumn near mossy areas and leaf litter.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The distinctive dark-edged gills of this species create the visual effect that gives it its common name 'Necklace Tricholoma,' making it one of the more visually recognizable Tricholoma species in the region.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
